usage=r'''
\section{Observed use: from capability to actual behavior}
A capability claim alone is inadequate evidence for population-level policy. Three different types of empirical observation establish use without requiring the researcher to visit abusive services, obtain images or reproduce abuse.
\fig{survey_usage}{.87}{Self-reported experiences and behaviors in the CHI 2024 ten-country study, over 16,000 respondents. Source: \cite{chi24}. Not a global prevalence estimate.}
The survey establishes that perpetrators as well as victims report real experiences, not only hypothetical intentions. In Australia, a recontact survey of children aged 10--17 found that 3\% of those answering sensitive questions reported that a fabricated nude depiction of them had been made without permission at some point; 1\% reported this within the prior year. Of 2,302 recontact respondents, 354 skipped the sensitive module, leaving 1,948. The percentages are rounded and are not model-specific. \cite{auskids}

A selected 31-site investigation estimated 20.7 million visits in May 2025. A separate study analyzed 29 commercial applications, including marketing and business practices that the authors argue normalize sexual image abuse. Visits measure demand or exposure proxies, not completed generations, unique individuals, profit or victims. The two samples may overlap; their counts cannot be added. \cite{isdtraffic,apps29}

\textbf{Direct distribution evidence.} Australian eSafety reported actual misuse of models downloaded from hosting platforms, including imagery involving real children and abuse survivors. Following regulatory engagement, Hugging Face amended its terms regarding model-publisher risk mitigation. This is stronger evidence of a relevant downstream risk than popularity or a model card alone. It does not quantify the fraction of abuse caused by downloadable weights or establish that all models in a family share the same risk. \cite{ausmodels}

\begin{center}\small\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}YYY@{}}\toprule
\textbf{Observation} & \textbf{Supported inference} & \textbf{Unsupported inference}\\\midrule
Self-reported perpetration & Actual behavior reported by respondents & Frequency for all populations or a causal model comparison\\
Estimated website visits & Material demand for selected services & Number of outputs, offenders or unique victims\\
Regulator attribution to downloaded models & Weight distribution is one documented abuse pathway & Share of all abuse caused by open models\\
School-related evidence & Safeguarding institutions need response pathways & Every school or every child is equally affected\\\bottomrule\end{tabularx}\end{center}
'''
tex=tex.replace(r'\section{Criminal cases: from possibility to established conduct}',usage+'\n'+r'\section{Criminal cases: from possibility to established conduct}',1)
intervention=r'''
\section{Real-world intervention evidence: what has actually changed}
The strongest policy argument distinguishes enforcement activity from changes in service behavior, and both from ultimate harm reduction. A law on the books is not a measured outcome. The following cases provide observable intermediate outcomes and a basis for a testable policy, not a claim that a ban has already eliminated abuse.

\begin{center}\small\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{.17\linewidth}YY@{}}\toprule
\textbf{Jurisdiction} & \textbf{Documented action or response} & \textbf{Evidence boundary}\\\midrule
Australia & Eight targeted services withdrew local access or introduced compliance measures; previously over 200,000 Australian visits/month combined. \cite{ausbill} & Service response observed; no quantified post-action victim outcome. The eight include earlier activity and must not be added to earlier withdrawal counts.\\
Australia & Model repository terms changed following regulator engagement; three hosted services withdrew access in an earlier action. \cite{ausmodels} & Policy revision and access changes, not proof of universal compliance.\\
Australia & Court ordered AUD 343,500 civil penalty plus costs for deepfake posting. \cite{auscourt} & Adjudicated penalty; not compensation or measured deterrence.\\
United States & FTC sent warnings to 12 companies in May 2026. \cite{ftcletters} & Apparent noncompliance, not final findings or confirmed remediation.\\
United Kingdom & Ofcom reported engagement with 25 high-risk services by October 2026; some were implementing protective measures. \cite{ofcomncii} & No completed-remediation denominator or measured victim impact.\\
International & Operation Cumberland led to 25 arrests of suspected distributors. \cite{cumberland} & Arrests are not convictions; no model attribution.\\\bottomrule\end{tabularx}\end{center}

The Australian November 2026 parliamentary submission specifically argues that removal powers should extend across relevant infrastructure. The submission also notes that restrictions on discovery alone cannot guarantee removal of the underlying service. This supports a multi-layer, legally reviewable control strategy rather than dependence on a single intermediary. The cited proposal is not itself law. \cite{ausbill}

\fig{governance_gap}{.83}{Ofcom's first-year review: 69 of 104 risk-assessment records did not name a responsible individual (66.35\%, author calculation). Source: \cite{ofcomrisk}. Selected services; broader online safety, not AI-specific.}
The absence of named ownership in reviewed records is a measurable governance weakness, not proof of deliberate negligence. A documented responsible executive, incident review process and independently checkable mitigation record should therefore be prerequisites for high-risk releases.

\sub{Attribution and competing explanations}
The evidence establishes harm, demand, specific downstream misuse and some regulator-induced service changes. It does not establish the causal reduction in harm from a blanket model ban, the proportion attributable to each model family, or the extent of substitution to other services. A prospective study should measure these quantities rather than assume them.
'''
tex=tex.replace(r'\section{An enforceable implementation framework}',intervention+'\n'+r'\section{An enforceable implementation framework}',1)
protocol=r'''
\section{Evidence-to-action framework and outcome evaluation}
Each control must be traceable to a documented failure mode, a responsible decision-maker and a measurable result. The primary endpoint is protection of people, not the number of press releases, removed accounts or model files.
\begin{center}\small\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{.18\linewidth}YY@{}}\toprule
\textbf{Control} & \textbf{Evidence and accountable actor} & \textbf{Outcome to measure}\\\midrule
C01: purpose-built prohibition & Commercial misuse and service demand; legislature and regulator. \cite{isdtraffic,apps29,ausbill} & Verified availability and substitution; independent victimization survey.\\
C02: release safety case & Documented downloadable-model misuse; publisher and repository. \cite{ausmodels,ntia} & High-risk releases independently reviewed; severe residual findings resolved before release.\\
C03: named risk owner & Missing ownership in Ofcom records; provider board and regulator. \cite{ofcomrisk} & Share of audited records with owner, approval and dated corrective actions.\\
C04: timely removal & FTC warnings and India duties; intermediaries and regulators. \cite{ftcletters,indiafull} & Valid reports acted upon within legal deadline; time to protection.\\
C05: duplicate prevention & Ongoing Ofcom engagement; platform safety teams. \cite{ofcomncii} & Repeat exposure at 7 and 30 days, false matches and appeal reversals.\\
C06: victim support & Survey and lived experience evidence; child protection and support services. \cite{auskids,deepfakeimpact} & Referral completion, reporting burden, perceived safety and access.\\\bottomrule\end{tabularx}\end{center}
\sub{Prospective evaluation design (not completed experiments)}
Register a protocol before analysis. Establish a baseline and monthly follow-up for at least 12 months where historical data allow. Compare treated services or jurisdictions with an appropriately matched comparison group, using aggregate authorized regulator or provider data. Record concurrent legal changes, changes to reporting channels, seasonality and service migration. Avoid claiming causal effects when pre-trends, selection or spillover undermine the comparison. Report missingness and denominators; use independent analyst review, and privacy-protect small cells.

Define deadline compliance as $N_{\mathrm{timely}}/N_{\mathrm{eligible}}$, with all eligible reports old enough to be evaluated, including unresolved cases. Define repeat exposure as $N_{\mathrm{repeat}}/N_{\mathrm{followed}}$ among resolved cases with adequate follow-up; report losses to follow-up. Compare incidence using repeated consent-based, representative surveys with identical wording and time windows. Record adverse outcomes including wrongful removal, delayed appeals and privacy intrusions. Publish uncertainty intervals only where data and sampling design support them. Reporting growth may reflect improved access, not greater underlying abuse.

\sub{A release gate with explicit evidence requirements}
Require a signed safety case for high-risk systems: provenance documentation, intended uses, independent authorized assessment, residual-risk register, named accountable officer, incident-response route, version-specific remediation evidence and review date. A credible unresolved critical finding triggers a proportionate temporary hold or controlled access pending review. Absence of evidence is not evidence of safety; nor is an unsupported allegation sufficient for a permanent prohibition. Provide procedural review, appeal and research exemptions under applicable law. Never require public reproduction of illegal images to substantiate a finding.

\sub{India: move from legal obligations to verifiable compliance}
The 2026 consolidated IT Rules distinguish a two-hour duty to take reasonable and practicable measures on qualifying complaints from affected individuals or their representatives (Rule 3(2)(b)) from a three-hour removal or disabling deadline following specified actual knowledge (Rule 3(1)(d)). Rule 3(3) addresses due diligence for unlawful synthetically generated information and provenance for other covered synthetic information. Rule 3A provides an appellate route. These provisions are not interchangeable and do not by themselves establish an across-the-board prohibition on model weights. \cite{indiafull}

Request anonymized, trigger-specific compliance data from MeitY and appropriate authorities; develop a child-safety referral protocol with NCPCR and State agencies; commission an independent review of purpose-built generator supply and cross-stack enforcement powers. A school pilot should evaluate response readiness with fictional, non-intimate materials and staff training only, not generate abusive images or recruit children to test harmful services. No such pilot is claimed to have been conducted.
'''
tex=tex.replace(r'\sect{Synthesis, limitations and conclusion}',protocol+'\n'+r'\sect{Synthesis, limitations and conclusion}',1)
